  "afd": {
   "issued_utc": "2026-07-25T17:40:00+00:00",
   "text": "\n000\nFXUS64 KEWX 251740\nAFDEWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Austin/San Antonio TX\n1240 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\n...New SHORT TERM, LONG TERM...\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\nUpdated at 1232 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\n- The Nueces River at Asherton continues to slowly recede within\n  minor flood stage.\n\n- A benign weather pattern for the week ahead with seasonable to \n  slightly above average temperatures and rain free conditions.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM...\n(Tonight through Sunday night)\nIssued at 1232 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nWeak easterly mid-level flow around the southern periphery of the\nSubtropical Ridge to our north will foster continued afternoon\ncumulus and at least a slim shot at showers over the Coastal\nPlains. For this afternoon/evening, will not carry any PoPs as \nslightly drier air and a weaker seabreeze will likely keep things \neast of our region. On Sunday, the ridge will shift slightly north\nfor a day, allowing a few showers and storms to develop over the\nCoastal Plains by late afternoon. Anything that forms will be very\npulsy, so accumulations will be minimal. Temperatures remain\ntoasty, with highs in the mid to upper 90s.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM...\n(Monday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1232 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nSubtropical Ridging will remain in place over the western\nPanhandle and northeastern New Mexico Monday-Friday, resulting in\nincreasingly hot conditions. Humidity should mix out a bit more\neach afternoon, with a stretch of triple digit heat expected for\nmost locations Tuesday-Friday. By next weekend, ridging starts to\nslide west into the Four Corners, which could open the door for\nnortherly flow and some low end rain chances. For now, with it\nbeing nearly a week away, have opted for a mainly dry forecast in\nthe long term. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...\n(18Z TAFS)\nIssued at 1220 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nClouds continue to scatter with VFR conditions expected for the \nafternoon and evening. South to southeasterly wind will generally be \naround 8-12 knots through the evening becoming lighter overnight. \nMVFR ceilings likely develop for SAT/SSF later tonight through \nSunday morning, with less confidence if any BKN low ceilings impact \nKAUS. KDRT is expected to remain VFR. \n\n&&\n\n.PRELIMINARY POINT TEMPS/POPS...\nAustin Camp Mabry              75  98  76  98 /   0   0   0   0 \nAustin Bergstrom Intl Airport  75  97  76  98 /   0   0   0   0 \nNew Braunfels Muni Airport     74  97  75  98 /   0   0   0   0 \nBurnet Muni Airport            71  96  73  97 /   0   0   0   0 \nDel Rio Intl Airport           77  99  78 101 /   0   0   0   0 \nGeorgetown Muni Airport        74  97  76  98 /   0   0   0   0 \nHondo Muni Airport             73  95  75  98 /   0   0   0   0 \nSan Marcos Muni Airport        74  97  74  98 /   0   0   0   0 \nLa Grange - Fayette Regional   75  97  77  99 /   0  20   0   0 \nSan Antonio Intl Airport       75  96  76  97 /   0   0   0   0 \nStinson Muni Airport           76  96  77  98 /   0   0   0   0 \n\n&&\n\n.EWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nNone.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...MMM\nLONG TERM....MMM\nAVIATION...27\n"
